<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Douai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Douai is a town on the river Scarpe in the Nord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was one of the great cloth towns of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>; it passed to the French crown in 1312, returned to Flanders in 1369 and became French for good when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> took it in 1667. Its university, founded in 1559, was a centre of Catholic learning, and its English College produced the Douay Bible.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/douai.jpg" alt="Douai" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JackyM59 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Fontaine_de_la_Place_d%27Armes_de_Douai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
